{"plan_objective_id": 1, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 1,
  "objective": "Research BN coating chemistry, binders, and failure mechanisms with reactive melts (Al-containing Cu alloys)",
  "rationale": "Need to understand why C63000 skull bonds to and pulls off BN coating - binder reactions, wetting, thermal mismatch",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found key evidence: BN coating binders include phosphate (harden 800-900°C, brittle), Al2O3 (>1000°C), Al2O3-SiO2 (<1000°C), clay. Al wets BN above ~900-1000°C (contact angle ~160° below 900°C, excellent wetting at 1000°C). Al-BN shear strength 6.1±2.0 MPa. Al wets graphite strongly above 1150°C with Al4C3 formation. Thermal expansion mismatch: BN 0.8-7.5×10⁻⁶, graphite 2-20×10⁻⁶, Cu alloy ~16-18×10⁻⁶.",
  "evaluation": "Good evidence from Rudolph 2013, Nicholas 1990, Pastukhov 2006 on all key aspects."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 2, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 2,
  "objective": "Find vendor guidance and best practices for BN coating application on graphite crucibles",
  "rationale": "Need specific product recommendations, coating procedures, cure schedules, and thickness targets",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found Rudolph 2013 guidance on BN coatings. Phosphate binders harden at 800-900°C. Water or alcohol carriers. Spray gun with 1.5mm nozzle recommended. Thin layers needed. Recoat after each casting. For high-expansion substrates, use Al2O3 or Al2O3-SiO2 binders, not phosphate. Additional vendor-specific data (ZYP, Henze) not found in literature but general principles established.",
  "evaluation": "Reasonable coverage from available sources. Vendor-specific data sheets not found in academic literature but general coating guidance is solid."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 3, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 3,
  "objective": "Research wetting of graphite by Cu-Al melts, Al4C3 formation, carbon pickup, and contamination effects",
  "rationale": "Need to understand what bare graphite patches do to C63000 melt and whether debris could cause nozzle blockage",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found: Al wets graphite above 1150°C with Al4C3 formation. At 1200°C, Al on graphite contact angle 85° after 5 min. Carbon has very limited solubility in Cu (~8 ppm below 800°C). Al4C3 thermodynamically favorable below 2100°C. Formation reaction: 4Al + 3C → Al4C3 (ΔG = -47.2 kJ/g-atom C). Al4C3 hydrolyzes in water. Standard Gibbs energy decreasing sequence: Ti>Al>Si for carbide formation.",
  "evaluation": "Good quantitative data from Pastukhov 2006 and Zare 2026. Cu-Al specific data limited but pure Al data highly relevant."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 4, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 4,
  "objective": "Research skull formation, prevention, and removal in induction melting of copper alloys",
  "rationale": "Need practical guidance on draining, pressure, crucible design, and skull removal without damaging coating",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Some evidence found on skull formation in various melting contexts. Key insights: proper crucible drainage angle, maintaining melt temperature during drain, end-of-pour gas pressure, and avoiding mechanical damage to coatings. Limited specific literature on copper alloy skull management in small atomizers.",
  "evaluation": "Partially addressed through general foundry principles. Specific guidance will be synthesized from first principles and available evidence."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 5, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 5,
  "objective": "Research alternative crucible materials and coatings for aluminum bronze",
  "rationale": "Need to evaluate alternatives to sprayed BN on graphite for reactive Al-containing copper alloys",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Literature mentions alumina, zirconia, magnesia as crucible options for non-ferrous. BN has thermal stability advantage. Graphite crucibles standard for Cu alloys with induction coupling. Solid hBN and pyrolytic BN (PBN) are alternatives to sprayed coatings. Alumina and zirconia crucibles don't couple well with induction but can be used with susceptors.",
  "evaluation": "Reasonable coverage from available evidence and metallurgical principles."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 6, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 6,
  "objective": "Compile step-by-step recoat procedure and go/no-go checklist",
  "rationale": "Need to synthesize all findings into a practical procedure for the next run",
  "status": "in-progress",
  "result": null,
  "evaluation": null
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 6, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 6,
  "objective": "Compile step-by-step recoat procedure and go/no-go checklist",
  "rationale": "Need to synthesize all findings into a practical procedure for the next run",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Created comprehensive recoat procedure table (artifact-01) with 21 steps covering surface prep, coating selection, application, cure, inspection, and go/no-go criteria.",
  "evaluation": "Thorough procedure created integrating all evidence sources."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 7, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 7,
  "objective": "Create artifacts and submit final answer",
  "rationale": "Need to organize all findings into a comprehensive, well-cited answer",
  "status": "in-progress",
  "result": null,
  "evaluation": null
}"}

